WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.082 --> 00:00:01.650
the big moments from the supreme

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:06.055
court oral arguments today.

00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:08.323
&gt;&gt; Cancel student debt now!

00:00:08.457 --> 00:00:09.658
&gt;&gt; Reporter: Big stakes for more

00:00:09.725 --> 00:00:11.894
than 40 million student loan

00:00:12.027 --> 00:00:12.961
borrowers, as the supreme court

00:00:13.095 --> 00:00:15.197
decides whether a program

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.532
eliminating up to $20,000 in

00:00:16.665 --> 00:00:20.502
debt per borrower can go into

00:00:20.636 --> 00:00:20.836
effect.

00:00:20.969 --> 00:00:22.004
&gt;&gt; To provide a measure of loan

00:00:22.137 --> 00:00:23.172
forgiveness to make sure that

00:00:23.305 --> 00:00:24.907
this unprecedented pandemic does

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.676
not leave borrowers worse off in

00:00:27.676 --> 00:00:28.544
relation to their loans.

00:00:28.677 --> 00:00:29.278
&gt;&gt; Reporter: The Biden

00:00:29.678 --> 00:00:30.546
administration is defending

00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:31.814
their student loan program,

00:00:31.947 --> 00:00:33.682
arguing it is necessary in the

00:00:33.682 --> 00:00:34.383
wake of the covid pandemic.

00:00:34.516 --> 00:00:35.918
But the conservative justices

00:00:36.051 --> 00:00:38.087
repeatedly zeroed in on the

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.988
program's $400 billion plus

00:00:40.122 --> 00:00:42.157
price tag to question whether

00:00:42.291 --> 00:00:43.692
the president by way of his

00:00:43.692 --> 00:00:44.460
education secretary has the

00:00:44.593 --> 00:00:46.462
power to enact this kind of

00:00:46.595 --> 00:00:47.196
relief.

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:48.430
&gt;&gt; I think most casual observers

00:00:48.564 --> 00:00:50.299
would say if you're going to

00:00:50.432 --> 00:00:52.067
give up that much amount of

00:00:52.201 --> 00:00:55.137
money, if you're going to effect

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.905
the obligations of that many

00:00:57.039 --> 00:00:58.507
Americans on a subject that's of

00:00:58.640 --> 00:00:59.708
great controversy, they would

00:00:59.808 --> 00:01:00.409
think that's something for

00:01:00.542 --> 00:01:01.710
congress to act on.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:02.578
&gt;&gt; Reporter: The solicitor

00:01:02.711 --> 00:01:03.378
general responded that federal

00:01:03.712 --> 00:01:05.347
law allows for the education

00:01:05.714 --> 00:01:07.282
secretary to waive or modify

00:01:07.416 --> 00:01:10.586
loan obligations in the wake of

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:11.286
an emergency, and the ongoing

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:12.020
financial effects of the

00:01:12.154 --> 00:01:14.490
pandemic justifies the

00:01:14.623 --> 00:01:15.190
administration's stepping in.

00:01:15.324 --> 00:01:15.924
&gt;&gt; Without this critical relief

00:01:16.058 --> 00:01:17.226
for debtors, we are going to

00:01:17.359 --> 00:01:18.861
have a wave of default across

00:01:18.994 --> 00:01:21.130
the country with all of the

00:01:21.263 --> 00:01:21.964
negative consequences that has

00:01:22.097 --> 00:01:23.265
for borrowers.

00:01:23.398 --> 00:01:24.967
I think it is precisely the type

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.435
of context where the executive

00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:27.870
should be able to implement

00:01:28.003 --> 00:01:28.737
those emergency powers.

00:01:28.871 --> 00:01:29.738
&gt;&gt; Reporter: The supreme court

00:01:29.738 --> 00:01:30.906
has repeatedly struck down

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:33.275
programs implemented by the

00:01:33.408 --> 00:01:33.942
Biden administration under

00:01:34.076 --> 00:01:35.744
covid, including the eviction

00:01:35.744 --> 00:01:37.146
moratorium and the testing or

00:01:37.279 --> 00:01:39.181
vaccine mandate for large

00:01:39.314 --> 00:01:39.748
employers.

00:01:39.748 --> 00:01:40.682
This student debt program uses

00:01:40.816 --> 00:01:43.185
the pandemic to justify

00:01:43.318 --> 00:01:44.153
forgiving $10,000 in federal

00:01:44.286 --> 00:01:47.422
loans for people making under

00:01:47.756 --> 00:01:49.324
$125,000, or $20,000 in loans

00:01:49.758 --> 00:01:51.360
for those with Pell grants.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.862
About 20 million borrowers could

00:01:53.996 --> 00:01:55.864
see their remains balances

00:01:55.998 --> 00:01:57.432
entirely wiped out.

00:01:57.766 --> 00:01:59.301
But justice Neil Gorsuch asked

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:02.004
whether that undermines basic

00:02:02.137 --> 00:02:02.371
fairness.

00:02:02.504 --> 00:02:03.105
&gt;&gt; What I think they argue that

00:02:03.238 --> 00:02:04.306
is missing is cost to other

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:06.508
persons in terms of fairness,

00:02:06.642 --> 00:02:07.776
for example, people who have

00:02:07.776 --> 00:02:11.780
paid their loans, people who

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.183
plan their lives around not

00:02:14.316 --> 00:02:15.951
seeking loans, and people who

